        "answer": {
          "short": "Method 1 gives $\\frac12\\sin^2x+C$; method 2 gives $-\\frac14\\cos2x+D$.",
          "worked": "Method 1: Let $f(x)=\\sin x$, so $f'(x)=\\cos x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\int\\sin x\\cos x\\,dx&=\\frac{\\sin^2x}{2}+C.\\end{align*}$$\nMethod 2: Using $\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin x\\cos x&=\\frac12\\sin2x,\\\\\\int\\sin x\\cos x\\,dx&=\\int\\frac12\\sin2x\\,dx\\\\&=-\\frac14\\cos2x+D.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p64-q25-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\frac12\\sin^2x+C=-\\frac14\\cos2x+D$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=C+\\frac14$; the two forms differ by a constant.",
          "worked": "Using $\\sin^2x=\\frac12(1-\\cos2x)$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac12\\sin^2x+C&=\\frac14-\\frac14\\cos2x+C\\\\&=-\\frac14\\cos2x+\\left(C+\\frac14\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nSo $D=C+\\frac14$. The two answers differ by a constant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      }
